/*!
    \page style-aware-widgets.html
    \title Writing Style-Aware Widgets
    \brief How to paint a custom widget through the current style so that it
    looks right in every style.

    A style-aware widget conforms to the style in which it's drawn. Instead of
    painting frames, buttons, and indicators itself, it asks the current style
    to draw them, and it asks the style for the metrics it needs to lay out its
    contents. The widget then looks native on every platform, follows the
    application palette, and picks up a custom style or a style sheet like Qt's
    own widgets do.

    \section1 Build a style option

    The style draws from the information in a \l QStyleOption, so the first step
    in a paint event is to fill one out. Pick the option class that matches the
    element you want to draw, call \l QStyleOption::initFrom() to set the state
    flags, rectangle, palette, and font metrics that every widget shares, and
    then set the members that are specific to your element.

    Qt's widgets do this in a protected virtual \c initStyleOption() function,
    so that subclasses can adjust the option. Follow the same pattern in your
    widget.

    \section1 Draw through the style

    Draw the elements with the drawing functions of \l QStyle, passing the
    option, a \l QPainter, and the widget itself:

    \snippet styles/styles.cpp 2
    \dots
    \snippet styles/styles.cpp 3

    \l QStylePainter combines a \l QStyle, a \l QPainter, and a \l QWidget. Its
    drawing functions take the element and the option only, which makes the code
    shorter:

    \snippet styles/styles.cpp 5
    \dots
    \snippet styles/styles.cpp 7

    \image paintsystem-stylepainter.png
           {Diagram showing QStylePainter inherits from QPainter}

    Use the palette in the option, or \l QWidget::palette(), for any color you
    draw yourself, so that the widget follows the application palette and works
    in both light and dark color schemes.

    \section1 Ask the style for sizes

    Base your \l{QWidget::}{sizeHint()} and \l{QWidget::}{minimumSizeHint()} on
    the style. \l{QStyle::}{sizeFromContents()} turns the size of your contents
    into the size of the widget for a given contents type, and
    \l{QStyle::}{pixelMetric()} returns the style's frame widths, margins, and
    indicator sizes. Use \l{QStyle::}{subElementRect()} to find out where the
    style places the parts of an element, instead of computing the positions
    yourself.

    \section1 Report the widget's state

    The style draws hover and focus effects only if the state flags are set. Set
    the \l{Qt::}{WA_Hover} attribute on your widget to receive hover events, and
    add \c State_MouseOver to the option while the mouse is over the element.
    Set \c State_Sunken while the widget is pressed, and \c State_On,
    \c State_Off, or \c State_NoChange for checkable elements.
    \l{Widget Style Reference} lists the flags that each element expects.

    \section1 Follow style and palette changes

    When the application style or palette changes, the widget receives a
    \l QEvent::StyleChange or \l QEvent::PaletteChange event in
    \l{QWidget::}{changeEvent()}. Invalidate any cached metrics there and call
    \l{QWidget::}{updateGeometry()} if your size hint depends on the style.

    \section1 Support right-to-left layouts

    In a right-to-left layout, the style mirrors asymmetric elements. Use
    \l{QStyle::}{visualRect()} to convert a rectangle from logical to screen
    coordinates and \l{QStyle::}{visualAlignment()} to mirror an alignment, and
    pass the option's \c direction through to the style. Test with the
    \c -reverse command-line option.

    \sa QStyle, QStylePainter, QStyleOption, {How a Style Draws a Widget},
        {Widget Style Reference}
*/
